[{:rule-id "rule-ai", :item {:feed-id "hn", :item-id "https://www.polso.info/why-i-will-not-be-using-llms-coding", :title "I will not be using LLMs in my coding", :link "https://www.polso.info/why-i-will-not-be-using-llms-coding", :published-at #inst "2026-11-08T12:14:10.000-00:00", :content "Comments"}, :excerpts [{:text "I will not be using LLMs in my coding", :matched-terms ["llm"], :source :title}]} {:rule-id "clojure", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/vimslime", :title "vim-slime", :link "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/vimslime", :published-at #inst "2026-11-08T12:33:20.000-00:00", :content "<!DOCTYPE html> <html> <head> <title>Tarn Barford</title> <meta charset=\"utf-8\"/> <link rel=\"icon\" type=\"image/x-icon\" href=\"/favicon.ico\"> <link href=\"/style.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link rel=\"alternate\" type=\"application/atom+xml\" title=\"Journals of Tarn Barford\" href=\"/atom\" /> <link href=\"/highlight.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link href=\"/highlight-console.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> </head> <body> <div id=\"container\"> <div id=\"header\"> <div id=\"header\"> <p>From the <a href=\"/journal\">Journals</a> of <a href=\"/\">Tarn Barford</a></p> <h1> vim-slime </h1> <p> Mar 26, 2012 </p> </div> </div> <div id=\"post_content\"> <html><body><p>Today I found the awesomeness that is <a href=\"https://github.com/jpalardy/vim-slime\">vim-slime</a>, it's been an exciting day for me. <a href=\"http://common-lisp.net/project/slime/\">Slime</a> is the \"The Superior Lisp Interaction Mode for Emacs\", I can almost hear the emacs crowd laughing.</p> <p>For those that use vim and haven't used Slime, vim-slime or <a href=\"https://github.com/vim-scripts/VimClojure\">something similar</a>, this is why it's awesome:</p> <p><strong>Text can be sent from any process to the stdin of a <a href=\"http://www.gnu.org/software/screen/\">gnu screen</a> or <a href=\"http://tmux.sourceforge.net/\">tmux</a> session. The process in this case is vim and the screen/tmux session is a terminal</strong>.</p> <p>Screen is a <a href=\"/journal/oh-screen-where-have-you-been\">really neat</a> terminal multiplexer (you can run multiple terminals in a terminal window). The multiplexed shell processes are children of the screen process, which itself is not a child of the terminal window process. This means a screen process and its child processes keep running if you close the terminal window. Later you can re-connect to it, this is what makes vim-slime possible.</p> <p>Here is an screen shot, on the left is me in gVim writing some awful Clojure <a href=\"#footnote-1\">[1]</a>. On the right is a screen buffer in which I started a Clojure REPL. When I want to try run some code I can send any vim text selection to the REPL in a keystroke (or two).</p> <p><img alt=\"vim slime screenshot\" src=\"screenshot.jpg\"/></p> <p>It doesn't have to be a Clojure REPL either, we can send anything to a screen shell. We could run git commands, find, grep, sed, etc. Like with the Clojure REPL we can even interact with any terminal programs that use STDIN.</p> <p>This concept can be taken even further, You can even connect to a tmux session over SSH and share a terminal or a <a href=\"http://remotepairprogramming.com/remote-pair-programming-with-tmux-and-vim-the\">terminal program like vim to do remote pairing</a>!</p> <p>Hopefully remote pairing is the topic of my next post as there are a couple geographically distant people I know who are keen to do some pair hacking. I stand to learn a lot!</p> <p><a name=\"footnote-1\">[1]</a> I learnt almost everything I know about Lisp from <a href=\"http://www.ccs.neu.edu/home/matthias/BTLS/\">The Little Schemer</a>. Great book.</p></body></html> </div> <div id=\"comments\"> </div> </div> <div id=\"footer\"> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>Questions, comments, suggestions? Email me, <a href=\"mailto:tarn@tarnbarford.net\">tarn@tarnbarford.net</a> (<a href=\"/pgp.txt\">public key</a>)</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> </div> </body> </html>"}, :excerpts [{:text "... or <a href=\"https://github.com/vim-scripts/VimClojure\">something similar</a>, this is why it's...", :matched-terms ["clojure"], :source :content} {:text "... on the left is me in gVim writing some awful Clojure <a href=\"#footnote-1\">[1]</a>. On the right is a screen buffer in which I started a Clojure REPL. When I want to try run some code I can send any vim text selection to the REPL in a keystroke (or two).</p> <p><img alt=\"vim...", :matched-terms ["clojure" "repl"], :source :content} {:text "... <p>It doesn't have to be a Clojure REPL either, we can send anything to a screen shell. We could run git commands, find, grep, sed, etc. Like with the Clojure REPL we can even interact with any terminal programs...", :matched-terms ["clojure" "repl" "command"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "rescript", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/swipe", :title "Swipe Keyboard", :link "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/swipe", :published-at #inst "2026-11-08T12:33:20.000-00:00", :content "<!DOCTYPE html> <html> <head> <title>Tarn Barford</title> <meta charset=\"utf-8\"/> <link rel=\"icon\" type=\"image/x-icon\" href=\"/favicon.ico\"> <link href=\"/style.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link rel=\"alternate\" type=\"application/atom+xml\" title=\"Journals of Tarn Barford\" href=\"/atom\" /> <link href=\"/highlight.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link href=\"/highlight-console.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <style> #swipe-canvas { position: relative; width: 900px; height: 300px; } #swipe-results { font-size: 30px; padding-left: 50px; padding-left: 50px; } #swipe-results ul { margin: 0px; padding: 0px; } #swipe-results li { float: left; background-color: #DDDDDD; list-style-type: none; padding: 10px; margin: 5px; border-radius: 5px; } #swipe { position: relative; } #swipe-loading { position: absolute; height: 50px; width: 300px; top: 85px; left: 300px; background-color: darkgray; border-radius: 10px; text-align: center; padding-top: 20px; border: black; border-width: 5px; } </style> </head> <body> <div id=\"container\"> <div id=\"header\"> <div id=\"header\"> <p>From the <a href=\"/journal\">Journals</a> of <a href=\"/\">Tarn Barford</a></p> <h1> Swipe Keyboard </h1> <p> Apr 06, 2014 </p> </div> </div> <div id=\"post_content\"> <html><body><p>When I first tried a <a href=\"http://www.swype.com/\">Swype</a> keyboard I was impressed how effective it was. Even though I don't use the feature on my phone I was interested in how it could be built, so I <a href=\"https://github.com/tarnacious/swipe-keyboard\">implemented this otherwise useless swipe-able keyboard</a> below. It probably doesn't work on mobile devices, but works on modern browsers with mouse pointers (although I've only really tried Chrome and Firefox).</p> <div id=\"swipe\"> <canvas height=\"300px\" id=\"swipe-canvas\" width=\"900px\"></canvas> <div id=\"swipe-results\"></div> <div style=\"clear: both\"></div> <h2 id=\"swipe-loading\">Loading<noscript>Javascript is Required</noscript></h2> </div> <p>I initially tried to solve this using the technique Peter Norvig famously uses in his <a href=\"http://norvig.com/spell-correct.html]\">spell checker</a>. He takes a sequence of characters and generates a set of word candidates by adding, removing and swapping characters in the original sequence, the generated candidates are removed if they are not found a dictionary. This can work but to be effective too many combinations need to be generated.</p> <p>If the dictionary is indexed into a <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trie\">trie</a> the number of combinations generated can be reduced significantly by traversing the trie and only generating valid letter combinations. This is a pretty bare implementation of that, it requires: </p> <ul> <li>The first and last characters of the initial sequence are used </li> <li>Intermediate characters in the initial sequence can be repeated or discarded </li> <li>No characters are added or swapped</li> </ul> <p>Basically, if you swipe through all the characters in a word in order, then the word will be found if it is in the index regardless how many characters are swiped in between. It is surprisingly quick and effective.</p> <p>This implementation uses <a href=\"https://raw.github.com/first20hours/google-10000-english/master/google-10000-english.txt\">these 10000 words</a>, I intended to use digital books but never got around to it as these words demonstrate the concept well enough.</p> <p>This is the first thing I've written in <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">ClojureScript</a> or <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">Clojure</a>, so my code my vary from non-idiomatic to shamblolic. I initially used a <a href=\"http://clojuredocs.org/clojure_core/clojure.zip/zipper\">zipper</a> to build the trie with immutable data structures, but found the indexing took to long with my zipper implementation so I <a href=\"https://github.com/tarnacious/swipe-keyboard/commit/6edd7b26e78121fbe8586b3f0ef54ca8277d9e32\">switched to using native Javascript maps</a>.</p> <p>I found that <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/core.async\">core.async</a> library is really awesome, the <a href=\"http://docs.closure-library.googlecode.com/git/index.html\">Google closure library</a> and <a href=\"https://developers.google.com/closure/compiler/\">compiler</a> integration with <a href=\"http://leiningen.org/\">Leiningen</a> the <a href=\"https://github.com/emezeske/lein-cljsbuild\">cljsbuild plug-in</a> to be impressive. My main pains were the slow JVM start-up time, the advanced closure compiler build of the web worker script fails silently when run (but the main script works fine when compiled with the advanced compiler), and at times I felt some compile time type checking would be nice.</p> <p>I would like to extend this experiment to index the word occurrence counts and proceeding word counts in original text and rank the found words as most likely. Support casing, umlauts, special characters, spelling correction and compound words in the indexing and lookup. I think a live lookup while swiping would also be possible.</p> <p>Overall this was fun, turned out OK I think, and was a great learning experience.</p></body></html> </div> <div id=\"comments\"> </div> </div> <div id=\"footer\"> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>Questions, comments, suggestions? Email me, <a href=\"mailto:tarn@tarnbarford.net\">tarn@tarnbarford.net</a> (<a href=\"/pgp.txt\">public key</a>)</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> </div> <script src=\"swipe.js\" type=\"text/javascript\"></script> </body> </html>"}, :excerpts [{:text "... <h2 id=\"swipe-loading\">Loading<noscript>Javascript is Required</noscript></h2> </div> <p>I initially...", :matched-terms ["javascript"], :source :content} {:text "... in <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">ClojureScript</a> or <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">Clojure</a>, so my code my vary from...", :matched-terms ["rescript"], :source :content} {:text "... to using native Javascript maps</a>.</p> <p>I found that <a...", :matched-terms ["javascript"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "rule-ai", :item {:feed-id "sean-goedecke", :item-id "https://seangoedecke.com/llms-are-terrible-at-designing-ai-systems/", :title "LLMs are terrible at designing AI systems", :link "https://seangoedecke.com/llms-are-terrible-at-designing-ai-systems/", :published-at #inst "2026-11-08T00:00:00.000-00:00", :content "LLMs don’t know how to design software that uses LLMs. They’re comfortable writing normal programs, but if you throw an AI-shaped problem at them, they will usually screw it up. Why is that? Going down the symbolic rabbit hole For example, this post describes an attempt to vibe-code a memory tool that would flag when one memory contradicted another1. This is a great instance of a task that was virtually impossible five years ago but is now relatively trivial due to LLMs. You can simply task a LLM with scanning for contradictions, either in a big batch or pairwise2. However, when asked for the “state of the art” approach3, the AI agent in this case did not recommend LLMs. It started building a bewilderingly complex symbolic system to convert the memory items into structured logical atoms, then mechanically compare them with a solver. This, of course, did not and could not work, because there is no analytic way of determining whether any two natural-language sentences express the same proposition. The LLM knew this was impossible! When the user eventually lost patience and said “hey, why isn’t this working”, Claude immediately explained that this was a known 130-year-old problem with no solution. And yet it happily went around and around for weeks iterating on a dead end. I’ve run into this myself. When I was building Autodeck (AI-generated spaced repetition cards) my agents would routinely try to categorize cards by regex or keyword search instead of just making another LLM call. Why can’t LLMs use LLMs correctly? Why would this be happening? I have two obvious explanations and one deeper one. The first obvious explanation is that most training code is pre-LLMs. Assuming the labs aren’t feeding the current avalance of slop repositories directly into the models4, the vast majority of the code that models learn from was written before LLMs were an option. So it would make sense that they’d reach for non-LLM approaches, in the same way that they’d reach for jQuery over modern vanilla JS, or Backbone.js over React, right? Wrong, actually! LLMs don’t choose jQuery and Backbone. Whatever part of the training process pushes them towards up-to-date knowledge in other areas isn’t successfully pushing them towards LLMs, so this can’t be the whole story. The second obvious explanation is that RLVR is not a good environment to write code that calls LLMs. Much of the time LLMs spend training is spent in reinforcement learning environments where an automatic grader determines their performance (for instance, by running some code they wrote and checking the outputs). I don’t know this for a fact, but I would be surprised if these environments contained the credentials and API access for a LLM to call other LLMs5. So even if the pretrained LLM was comfortable writing that kind of code, the reinforcement learning process might discourage6 it from doing so. The third explanation, which I like the most, is that current AI agents are just not very good at software design. On this explanation, picking a doomed symbolic approach instead of simply calling a LLM is not a weird aberration but a normal instance of AI agents picking the wrong tool for the job. I’ve seen this behavior from LLMs in other contexts too: trying to build complex (and doomed) consensus algorithms instead of tolerating stale data, introducing synchronous checks in event-driven systems, and so on. In the past I’ve chalked these up to alignment, but I’m open to the idea that they’re just ordinary errors of software design. Conclusion One lesson I learn over and over again is that I can’t judge a LLM by the standards of a human. Any human programmer who was capable of implementing a sophisticated symbolic solver would know that it wouldn’t work, and so if they suggested that approach I’d assume7 they had a good reason. Only a LLM could be both smart enough and dumb enough to make this mistake. I write a lot about the skills that are involved in working effectively with LLMs. I still stand by what I wrote in February 2025: Using LLMs well involves extracting value from what they’re good at and avoiding wasting time at what they’re bad at. This is surprisingly hard, because of the point above. LLMs are good and bad seemingly at random: they will work hard for hours but sometimes be shockingly low-effort, they will draw on inhumanly broad expertise but fail to apply the most basic common-sense, they can successfully rewrite enormously complex programs but will often make very silly errors in design. If you can predict where they will do well or badly ahead of time, you can get enormous value from LLMs; if you can’t, you will likely waste a lot of time and tokens going down hopeless approaches. Here’s another example where the user “implied” that Opus 5.5 should use a judge model and it instead constructed a series of regex checks. ↩ Here I’d probably reach for a decision model. ↩ In hindsight, this was probably the key mistake, though I don’t know how you’d turn that into advice for non-technical vibe-coders. Never asking the agent about the state of the field is a mistake too. Put one more notch on the LLMs reward expertise belt. ↩ A fair assumption, I think: even though training on low-quality synthetic data clearly isn’t catastrophic for LLMs, it can’t be great. ↩ It would be expensive, slow, and risky. ↩ This theory also explains why LLMs are great at prompting other LLMs as subagents, or on training LLMs from scratch: they’re likely directly RL-ed and evaluated on that, unlike on writing code that calls LLMs. ↩ Well, okay, I wouldn’t — I’m arrogant enough to ask anyway — but I’d at least expect a good reason. ↩"}, :excerpts [{:text "LLMs are terrible at designing AI systems", :matched-terms ["llm"], :source :title} {:text "LLMs don’t know how to design software that uses LLMs. They’re comfortable writing normal programs,...", :matched-terms ["llm"], :source :content} {:text "... years ago but is now relatively trivial due to LLMs. You can simply task a LLM with scanning for contradictions, either in a big...", :matched-terms ["llm"], :source :content}]}]